{
 "description": "Frame data for the fighting scenario (T16), as data: every number a fight is resolved by, and none in code. One unit is one pixel; every position, speed and box is a whole number, so two runs cannot round differently. A frame is 1/60 s. A move's frames count from 1: `startup` frames before it can hit, then `active` frames when its hitbox is out, then `recovery` frames before its owner can act again. A box is two intervals: along the stage, and up from the floor -- a character is `tall` standing and `crouching_tall` crouching, and a move's `rise` is how far above its owner's feet its hitbox reaches, from bottom to top (below the feet, for a kick from the air). A move's `height` is what guard stops it: `high` is aimed above any crouch and is stopped standing, `mid` is stopped standing or crouching, `low` only crouching, `overhead` only standing, and a `throw` by neither. A `jump` is `takeoff` frames gathering on the ground -- when it cannot guard and can be thrown -- then an arc in whole frames and units: `rise` frames up to `height`, `hang` frames there, `fall` frames down, `speed` units a frame forwards or back, fixed at take-off, then `landing` frames before it can act, when it can be thrown. One move from the air a jump, and none once hit in the air. A move done in the air (`stance` `air`) hits as an overhead. A `backdash` (T40) is the evade: `frames` long from the press, `travel` frames of them going `speed` units a frame away from the way it faces, and on its first `invulnerable` frames nothing finds it -- no strike, no throw; after them, until it is over, it cannot guard and can be thrown.",
 "frames_per_second": 60,
 "stage": {
  "width": 640,
  "starts": [
